# No em-dashes in prose copy

Em-dashes (`—`) are a common AI-writing tell. Do not use them in user-facing prose.

## What to use instead

| Instead of `—` | Use |
|---|---|
| Introducing a list or elaboration | `:` (colon) |
| Parenthetical aside | `,` (comma) |
| Closely related independent clause | `;` (semicolon) |
| Natural sentence break | `.` (period, split into two sentences) |

## Allowed exceptions

- **Brand title separator**: `Deeper Global — [Page Title]` in `<title>` and OG image alt only
- **Data field display**: `— {source.publisher}` or `— {modality.also_known_as[0]}` (visual separator between structured data fields, not prose)
- **Code comments**: JSDoc / inline developer comments

## Examples

```
❌  'A guided atlas of therapy approaches — what each one is for...'
✅  'A guided atlas of therapy approaches: what each one is for...'

❌  'Design is trust infrastructure — not decoration.'
✅  'Design is trust infrastructure, not decoration.'

❌  'concerns — not a diagnosis'
✅  'concerns, not a diagnosis'

❌  'pre-qualify fit — so inquiries match the work'
✅  'pre-qualify fit, so inquiries match the work'
```

## DB content pipeline

Answer content in Supabase is sanitized during the rewrite pipeline (step 2):

```
npm run content:sanitize-staging-emdash -- --apply
```

Run this after any AI rewrite batch before promoting. For live fields already promoted, run the audit SQL in `scripts/audit-live-emdash.sql`.
